      "narrative": null,
      "content": "Annex F. Public Private Partnerships (PPPs) » page 62\nAnnex F\nPublic-private partnerships (PPPs)\nWhat is a PPP? \nA PPP represents a partnership between the public sector and the private sector for the purposes \nof designing, planning, financing, constructing and/or operating projects which would be \nregarded traditionally as falling within the remit of the public sector. \nWhy are PPPs used?\nGovernments may be attracted to a PPP arrangement for a variety of reasons. These commonly \ninclude:\n▶\n▶greater value for money than the public sector comparator\n▶\n▶sharing or transfer of risk to private-se",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Annex F defines a public-private partnership as public and private sectors working together to design, finance, build or operate projects normally run by the public sector, and lists reasons governments use them, such as value for money, risk transfer and budget limits. It describes common arrangements on a public-private continuum, from operation and maintenance contracts through lease, develop and operate to build-own-operate-transfer and related models. It sets out considerations for using such partnerships in large eHealth programmes, including short technology asset lives, high set-up costs, local market capacity, performance and payment regimes and tax treatment, and stresses that they differ from simply involving the private sector through outsourcing.",
